WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:20.008
Laurie and Grandpa were reading quietly together on the couch, when Laurie suddenly jumped up.

2
00:00:20.009 --> 00:00:25.546
"I almost forgot! Our teacher gave us each a tulip bulb today.

3
00:00:25.547 --> 00:00:33.639
She said if we plant it now, it should bloom in the spring." Laurie reached into her backpack.

4
00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:43.224
"Perfect. I was just reading some poetry by an eighteenth-century Turkish poet," Grandpa said, smiling dreamily.

5
00:00:43.225 --> 00:00:50.694
Laurie stared at her grandpa. "I said I got a tulip bulb today."

6
00:00:50.695 --> 00:00:55.068
Grandpa turned to her with the dreamy smile still on his face.

7
00:00:55.069 --> 00:00:59.774
"Do you know where tulips come from?" he asked.

8
00:00:59.775 --> 00:01:01.571
"Holland?"

9
00:01:01.572 --> 00:01:13.560
"Nope. Turkey. And the word 'tulip' comes from the Turkish word for turban because tulip bulbs and buds look like turbans, don't they?"

10
00:01:13.561 --> 00:01:19.071
Laurie smiled as she examined the bulb in her hand. "You're right!"

11
00:01:19.072 --> 00:01:27.730
"The period from 1718 to 1730 is known in Ottoman history as the Tulip Era.

12
00:01:27.731 --> 00:01:35.431
It was a time of peace for the Turkish Ottoman Empire, when the arts, culture, and architecture flourished.

13
00:01:35.432 --> 00:01:42.231
And—your bulb reminded me of this—tulip mania swept over the empire.

14
00:01:42.232 --> 00:01:51.080
Everyone, including the ruler, Sultan Ahmed III, was absolutely obsessed with tulips."

15
00:01:51.081 --> 00:01:56.954
"How funny," Laurie said. "So is a sultan like a king?"

16
00:01:56.955 --> 00:02:01.954
"Yes, a sultan is the ruler of a Muslim empire.

17
00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:12.950
The Ottoman Empire began in 1300 when a Muslim prince named Osman claimed a small area of land in western Turkey.

18
00:02:12.951 --> 00:02:21.021
Osman fought to expand his territory, and after he died, his son continued the expansion.

19
00:02:21.022 --> 00:02:29.006
Many years and many wars later, the Ottoman Empire had conquered neighboring Muslim dynasties in the Middle East,

20
00:02:29.007 --> 00:02:32.740
and most of the Eastern Roman Empire too.

21
00:02:32.741 --> 00:02:39.490
By the seventeenth century, the Ottomans controlled parts of Europe, Africa, and Asia.

22
00:02:39.491 --> 00:02:46.131
And the empire was always ruled by a sultan who was a male descendant of Osman.

23
00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:57.254
Sultans had luxurious lives. They lived in the best houses, wore the finest clothes, and ate the most delicious foods in the empire.

24
00:02:57.255 --> 00:03:01.457
They were also extremely sheltered from their people.

25
00:03:01.458 --> 00:03:12.390
In the 1400s Sultan Mehmed II conquered the city of Istanbul, made it the capital, and ordered a huge palace to be built there.

26
00:03:12.391 --> 00:03:22.162
The palace was called Topkapi. It was like a mini city, with multiple buildings, courtyards, mosques, and towers.

27
00:03:22.163 --> 00:03:30.061
This huge palace kept the sultan almost completely separated from his officials, soldiers, and subjects.

28
00:03:30.062 --> 00:03:41.925
But things change from generation to generation, and in the 1700s Sultan Ahmed III didn't like feeling locked up in stuffy, old Topkapi.

29
00:03:41.926 --> 00:03:45.250
He loved to celebrate and have parties.

30
00:03:45.251 --> 00:03:51.702
And he wanted to do this outside the palace. His grand vizier at the time—"

31
00:03:51.703 --> 00:03:55.496
"What's a grand vizier?" Laurie interrupted.

32
00:03:55.497 --> 00:04:01.816
"The grand vizier was the highest official in the Ottoman Empire after the sultan.

33
00:04:01.817 --> 00:04:07.317
He represented the sultan and was also the ruler's main adviser.

34
00:04:07.318 --> 00:04:13.996
The grand vizier was so important that only the sultan could hire or fire him.

35
00:04:13.997 --> 00:04:24.599
So the grand vizier suggested that the sultan build new royal palaces, along with mosques and public fountains that could be enjoyed by everyone.

36
00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:30.191
Not only would this make the sultan happy, but it would make him popular!

37
00:04:30.192 --> 00:04:34.352
You've heard of the French Palace of Versailles, haven't you?"

38
00:04:34.353 --> 00:04:41.870
"Yep. Marie Antoinette lived there!" Laurie said, proud to know something important.

39
00:04:41.871 --> 00:04:48.230
"Apparently Sultan Ahmed III was fascinated by the culture of Versailles.

40
00:04:48.231 --> 00:04:54.843
He had heard of the fancy parties and great banquets there.

41
00:04:54.844 --> 00:04:59.564
He wanted to bring a bit of that to the Ottoman Empire.

42
00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:04.186
So he built fountains and mosques that could be lit up at night.

43
00:05:04.187 --> 00:05:08.062
And he built a new palace with beautiful gardens.

44
00:05:08.063 --> 00:05:11.025
The gardens were of course filled with tulips—

45
00:05:11.026 --> 00:05:20.337
tulips with round petals, tulips with pointed petals, tulips with brilliant yellow petals, and tulips streaked red and purple.

46
00:05:20.338 --> 00:05:28.648
Acrobats and entertainers performed in these gardens, while artists sketched, and poets wrote beneath the stars.

47
00:05:28.649 --> 00:05:40.581
And sometimes, on really special nights, the royal gardeners strapped candles onto the backs of turtles and released them in between the rows of tulips.

48
00:05:40.582 --> 00:05:45.059
The colorful flowers were illuminated all night."

49
00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:50.751
"That sounds like fun," Laurie said. "Were kids allowed to go?"

50
00:05:50.752 --> 00:05:56.744
"I'm sure the royal children were. At least until their bedtime.

51
00:05:56.745 --> 00:06:02.919
Sounds like a happy time to be living in the Ottoman Empire, doesn't it?" Grandpa asked.

52
00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:07.250
"Only if you're invited to the parties!" Laurie said.

53
00:06:07.251 --> 00:06:14.954
"Clever girl," Grandpa said, nodding. "Naturally the entire empire was not invited.

54
00:06:14.955 --> 00:06:22.932
And even though it was one of the wealthiest periods in Ottoman history, not everyone enjoyed such luxury.

55
00:06:22.933 --> 00:06:30.622
Peasants who were struggling to earn a living each day were unhappy. And so were the janissaries."

56
00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:34.093
"Who were the janissaries?" Laurie asked.

57
00:06:34.094 --> 00:06:43.257
"They were the Ottoman Empire's soldiers. During the empire's periods of conquest, they were quite respected.

58
00:06:43.258 --> 00:06:47.327
But in peace time, they didn't have anything to do!

59
00:06:47.328 --> 00:06:52.639
Some became gardeners or craftsmen, and others became restless.

60
00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:59.703
Eventually war broke out in the 1720s on the empire's borders with Iran.

61
00:06:59.704 --> 00:07:06.655
Ottoman troops went to Iran, but they failed to settle anything, and the Ottomans lost some territory.

62
00:07:06.656 --> 00:07:13.337
Unhappy with the sultan's weak military response, a group of janissaries revolted.

63
00:07:13.338 --> 00:07:18.775
The sultan was forced to resign and had the grand vizier executed.

64
00:07:18.776 --> 00:07:26.507
For a few weeks, there was chaos as the rebels burned palaces and destroyed signs of excess wealth.

65
00:07:26.508 --> 00:07:34.086
Finally the new sultan, Mahmud, put an end to the rebellion, but the happy times were over.

66
00:07:34.087 --> 00:07:44.914
Mahmud was occupied with wars against Iran and Russia for the rest of his reign, and didn't have much time for fancy garden parties."

67
00:07:44.915 --> 00:07:51.304
"No more tulips for the Ottoman Empire?" Laurie asked, looking down at the bulb in her palm.

68
00:07:51.305 --> 00:07:57.305
"Well, tulips are still a pride of Turkey and appear in lots of Turkish art.

69
00:07:57.306 --> 00:08:02.155
We could call them a legacy of Sultan Ahmed III's reign."

70
00:08:02.156 --> 00:08:05.078
Laurie put the bulb on top of her head.

71
00:08:05.079 --> 00:08:17.404
"I'm going to go plant my turban now," she said, giggling. "See you later, Grandpa."
